Refactor this code by replacing the hand-written __new__ singleton with a module-level instance pattern:

class Settings:
    _inst = None
    def __new__(cls):
        if cls._inst is None:
            cls._inst = super().__new__(cls)
            cls._inst.data = {}
        return cls._inst

output:
from dataclasses import dataclass, field

@dataclass
class Settings:
    data: dict = field(default_factory=dict)

settings = Settings()   # single shared instance, explicit at import

What changed: the __new__ singleton boilerplate became a plain dataclass plus one named module instance; testability improves because you can construct fresh Settings() when needed.
